1
00:00:00,349 --> 00:00:03,566
Welcome to Stamp Page Designer, version eight.

2
00:00:03,616 --> 00:00:07,472
It's a free tool for making your own stamp album pages.

3
00:00:07,522 --> 00:00:16,632
You place every stamp, heading and story box yourself, and every stamp frame prints at its true size, so your stamps fit exactly.

4
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:21,163
To start a new album, choose the margins first.

5
00:00:21,213 --> 00:00:28,108
Classic margins are one inch at the sides, and about one and a quarter inches at the top and bottom.

6
00:00:28,158 --> 00:00:32,830
Dense margins are half an inch all round, which gives you more room.

7
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:37,275
If your pages go into a ring binder, tick the punch-hole margin.

8
00:00:37,325 --> 00:00:39,567
It adds half an inch on the left.

9
00:00:40,883 --> 00:00:42,390
Next, pick the paper.

10
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:50,845
There is Letter, A4, Legal and A3, and twelve album page formats, such as Scott, Minkus, Davo and Stanley Gibbons.

11
00:00:50,895 --> 00:00:56,259
Then choose the spacing between items, across the page and down the page.

12
00:00:56,309 --> 00:01:01,376
It goes from Tight, five millimetres, up to Wide, thirty millimetres.

13
00:01:01,426 --> 00:01:08,199
The box below shows exactly how big your usable area is, and how many grid squares it holds.

14
00:01:09,516 --> 00:01:14,743
Give the album a name, choose how many pages to start with, and press Start designing.

15
00:01:16,049 --> 00:01:17,307
This is the editor.

16
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:24,326
Along the top, you'll find the album name, Undo and Redo, Help, Save album, Download album, and Print.

17
00:01:24,376 --> 00:01:27,078
Below that is a row of coloured buttons.

18
00:01:27,128 --> 00:01:30,450
There's one for everything you can add to a page.

19
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,717
Hover over any button, and a short tip tells you what it does.

20
00:01:36,049 --> 00:01:38,280
Your pages are listed on the left.

21
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:40,493
The page itself is in the middle.

22
00:01:40,543 --> 00:01:46,127
And the panel on the right always shows the settings for whatever you have selected.

23
00:01:46,177 --> 00:01:49,682
When nothing is selected, it shows the page settings.

24
00:01:50,983 --> 00:01:53,159
Let's start with a Page Header.

25
00:01:53,209 --> 00:01:54,308
Type your title.

26
00:01:54,358 --> 00:01:55,529
Then pick a font.

27
00:01:55,579 --> 00:02:04,936
There are eighteen built in, and each one is shown in its own lettering, from formal scripts and handwriting to classic book faces.

28
00:02:04,986 --> 00:02:08,240
Then set the size, and make it bold or italic.

29
00:02:09,549 --> 00:02:11,935
Choose a height for the header box.

30
00:02:11,985 --> 00:02:18,269
It can be as low as two centimetres, which is new in version eight, up to four centimetres.

31
00:02:18,319 --> 00:02:22,445
Fit the width to the text, and give it a border if you like.

32
00:02:22,495 --> 00:02:31,283
Once the box is narrower than the page, you can place it in one of five positions: far left, left, centre, right, or far right.

33
00:02:32,616 --> 00:02:33,722
Now the stamps.

34
00:02:33,772 --> 00:02:39,961
Small, Medium, Large and Extra Large each add a stamp frame of exactly that size.

35
00:02:40,011 --> 00:02:43,812
Each one lands in the first free spot on the page.

36
00:02:45,116 --> 00:02:48,518
Every stamp gets a number: S1, S2, S3, and so on.

37
00:02:48,568 --> 00:02:53,027
The number stays with the stamp, and it helps you find it later.

38
00:02:53,077 --> 00:02:55,845
It shows on screen, but it never prints.

39
00:02:55,895 --> 00:03:04,581
When you have twenty stamps on a page, these numbers make it easy to talk about one of them, or to find it in the inventory.

40
00:03:05,883 --> 00:03:10,659
Select a stamp to type a caption under it, such as a catalogue number.

41
00:03:10,709 --> 00:03:14,382
Tick Multiple lines, and you can use up to four lines.

42
00:03:14,432 --> 00:03:22,863
You can also type a short note inside the frame, like the value of the stamp, and tick Print it if you want it on the page.

43
00:03:22,913 --> 00:03:25,483
The frame line can be dashed or solid.

44
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:31,964
And new in version eight, it can also be perforated, with little teeth like a real stamp edge.

45
00:03:32,014 --> 00:03:34,791
Strips gives a mount with a wider border.

46
00:03:36,116 --> 00:03:38,955
Every stamp can be resized with the mouse.

47
00:03:39,005 --> 00:03:41,981
Select it, and drag one of its blue corners.

48
00:03:42,031 --> 00:03:48,997
It grows and shrinks in steps of one and a quarter millimetres, up to two hundred and ten millimetres.

49
00:03:49,047 --> 00:03:51,748
It never goes past the edge of the page.

50
00:03:51,798 --> 00:03:54,155
If you go too far, just press Undo.

51
00:03:55,483 --> 00:03:58,950
The description box is for your story about a stamp.

52
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,834
Type your text, choose a size up to thirty points, and resize the box from its corners.

53
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:11,737
Clip Art opens a chooser first, so nothing lands on the page until you pick something.

54
00:04:11,787 --> 00:04:21,374
There are twenty-three line drawings in any colour, twenty classic stamp drawings, and two hundred and fifty-five country flags, with a search box.

55
00:04:21,424 --> 00:04:24,848
You can also use your own picture from your computer.

56
00:04:26,149 --> 00:04:28,378
To move anything, just drag it.

57
00:04:28,428 --> 00:04:30,583
It moves in half-square steps.

58
00:04:30,633 --> 00:04:35,729
When it comes near another item, its edges and centres pull into line.

59
00:04:35,779 --> 00:04:39,772
Pink lines show you why, and blue numbers show the gap.

60
00:04:39,822 --> 00:04:43,080
To drag freely, hold Alt, or Option on a Mac.

61
00:04:44,383 --> 00:04:49,825
Copy and Paste put a copy right next to the original, exactly one spacing away.

62
00:04:49,875 --> 00:04:52,397
You can even paste onto another page.

63
00:04:52,447 --> 00:04:59,835
Copy keeps the caption and the size, so it's the quickest way to add a row of stamps that are all the same.

64
00:05:01,149 --> 00:05:04,468
If items get too close, or overlap, they turn red.

65
00:05:04,518 --> 00:05:08,577
One line above the page tells you how many items need a look.

66
00:05:08,627 --> 00:05:10,867
Click it, and you'll see each one.

67
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:14,105
Auto-align fixes it for you.

68
00:05:14,155 --> 00:05:18,542
It evens out the spaces, and it puts every edge on a grid line.

69
00:05:18,592 --> 00:05:25,656
Pictures and text stay where you put them, and a frame moves as one piece, with everything inside it.

70
00:05:25,706 --> 00:05:28,474
Auto-align never shrinks a single stamp.

71
00:05:29,783 --> 00:05:31,966
Frames go round a set of stamps.

72
00:05:32,016 --> 00:05:34,967
Select a few stamps, and press Frame these.

73
00:05:35,017 --> 00:05:39,712
The frame fits round them, two and a half or five millimetres clear.

74
00:05:39,762 --> 00:05:46,062
Choose one, two or three lines, or a stamp edge with real perforation teeth, in any colour.

75
00:05:46,112 --> 00:05:48,784
Everything inside moves with the frame.

76
00:05:50,116 --> 00:05:53,696
New in version eight, the tab has a blue corner too.

77
00:05:53,746 --> 00:05:55,859
Drag it to make the tab bigger.

78
00:05:55,909 --> 00:06:03,607
It can be up to two centimetres tall, half outside the border and half inside, and up to nine centimetres wide.

79
00:06:03,657 --> 00:06:05,911
It's never smaller than its text.

80
00:06:05,961 --> 00:06:11,564
The line round it is now thin, or medium if you prefer, and it can be any colour.

81
00:06:12,883 --> 00:06:16,289
Preview shows the page exactly as it will print.

82
00:06:16,339 --> 00:06:18,592
There's no grid, and no colours.

83
00:06:18,642 --> 00:06:21,616
You can zoom in and out whenever you like.

84
00:06:21,666 --> 00:06:24,208
Zoom never changes the printed size.

85
00:06:25,516 --> 00:06:30,460
New in version eight, Add page opens a small gallery of page layouts.

86
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:32,414
Blank page is always first.

87
00:06:32,464 --> 00:06:45,080
Then there are six ready-made layouts: a country page, a definitive set, a single stamp with its story, a set of four, a souvenir sheet with singles, and a pocket sheet style.

88
00:06:45,130 --> 00:06:49,495
Each one is drawn for your album's paper, margins and border.

89
00:06:50,816 --> 00:06:55,272
Pick one, and the new page goes in right after the current page.

90
00:06:55,322 --> 00:06:57,525
Everything on it can be changed.

91
00:06:57,575 --> 00:07:01,891
Captions start empty, and the stamp numbers start again at S1.

92
00:07:03,216 --> 00:07:06,746
The page border decorates every page of the album.

93
00:07:06,796 --> 00:07:17,485
Choose from twenty-six patterns and twenty-four corners, such as the Ashoka Chakra, a lotus, a rose, a seated lion, a beaver, a kangaroo, or an eagle.

94
00:07:17,535 --> 00:07:20,778
Pick any colour you like, or no border at all.

95
00:07:22,083 --> 00:07:30,560
If you already keep your stamps in a spreadsheet, you can import the whole list, instead of adding the boxes one by one.

96
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:32,905
Start with Download example file.

97
00:07:32,955 --> 00:07:34,965
It opens in Excel or Numbers.

98
00:07:35,015 --> 00:07:38,021
It has every column, and a few sample rows.

99
00:07:38,071 --> 00:07:46,050
Only three columns are required: the ID, which is the catalogue number, the width, and the height in millimetres.

100
00:07:47,383 --> 00:07:50,725
Now press Import stamp list, and choose your file.

101
00:07:50,775 --> 00:07:57,443
The app puts up to eight stamps on a page, fewer when they don't fit, and it never shrinks a stamp.

102
00:07:57,493 --> 00:08:02,668
The new pages go after your current pages, so nothing you've made is touched.

103
00:08:02,718 --> 00:08:04,635
Each page is aligned for you.

104
00:08:04,685 --> 00:08:09,181
If you change your mind, Undo import takes it all back in one step.

105
00:08:10,483 --> 00:08:14,116
Inventory shows every stamp in the album, in one table.

106
00:08:14,166 --> 00:08:20,678
You'll see the page, the key, the catalogue number, the caption, the size, and whether you own it.

107
00:08:20,728 --> 00:08:22,419
The counts are at the top.

108
00:08:22,469 --> 00:08:24,695
Click a heading to sort the table.

109
00:08:24,745 --> 00:08:28,579
Click a row, and the app takes you straight to that stamp.

110
00:08:28,629 --> 00:08:30,655
The inventory is never printed.

111
00:08:31,983 --> 00:08:35,477
As an album grows, finding one stamp gets harder.

112
00:08:35,527 --> 00:08:39,890
So version eight has a Find a stamp box, just above the page.

113
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:44,375
Type part of a catalogue number or a caption, and press Enter.

114
00:08:44,425 --> 00:08:47,702
If several stamps match, you get a short list.

115
00:08:47,752 --> 00:08:53,416
Click one, and the app goes to its page, selects the stamp, and makes it flash.

116
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:59,854
You can also type a key, like three, underscore, S one, which means page three, stamp S1.

117
00:09:01,183 --> 00:09:04,269
Your work is kept automatically for Continue.

118
00:09:04,319 --> 00:09:09,357
To keep an album for good, press Save album, and pick one of three slots.

119
00:09:09,407 --> 00:09:16,327
Download album saves it as a file, with your pictures and layouts inside, so you always have a copy.

120
00:09:17,649 --> 00:09:20,301
When you're ready, press Save as PDF.

121
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:25,777
The app makes the PDF itself, at true size, and saves it to your Downloads.

122
00:09:25,827 --> 00:09:32,275
If two stamps overlap, nothing is saved, and the app takes you straight back to fix them.

123
00:09:32,325 --> 00:09:35,925
Print the PDF at one hundred percent, actual size.

124
00:09:37,250 --> 00:09:40,171
That's Stamp Page Designer, version eight.

125
00:09:40,221 --> 00:09:44,629
It's free, it's open source, and it runs right in your browser.

126
00:09:44,679 --> 00:09:45,832
Happy collecting!
